# Manuscript PDF page 88

                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Ellos cuentan. Nosotros recordamos.”
   They count. We remember.
   “Cuando te toque,” she said, “te acuerdas.”
   When it touches you, you’ll remember.
   The boy handed her the mango pit, like a gift. She laughed. And
then the wall was gone and the bridge was a bridge, and Wilfredo was
saying, “Hey, you’re crying, you good, you want a napkin,” and hand‐
ing me a napkin from a stack that looked like it had been prepared for
exactly this.
   I’d forgotten every word. The boy had forgotten by breakfast. I’d just
never known that the man had been carrying them anyway.



Abuela’s house is in the old city, on a street so narrow the cab had to
fold its mirrors, behind a wall the color of dried mango with bougain‐
villea spilling over the top in purple that doesn’t look real. The door
was green. I lifted my hand to knock.
   It opened.
   She was smaller than I remembered, and exactly as I remembered,
both at once, the way the heat had been. Seventy-nine. White hair, more
of it gone since the photos. My mother’s eyes. Mine. A dish towel over
one shoulder, as if she’d stepped away from the stove to open a door
she knew was about to be knocked on.
   She looked at my face. Then my hands, which were empty. Then my
face again, and put her palm on my cheek.
   “Flaco,” said my grandmother.
   That was all. She stepped back and I went in.
   The house smelled like every important day of my childhood, in a
kitchen I’d seen once. Sancocho, hours of it, yuca and plátano and three
kinds of meat, started before I landed, because she’d known. I sat
where she pointed. She wouldn’t let me help. She fed me, and watched
me eat the way my mother watches me eat, which is to say like a wo‐
man checking a pulse.
   “Abuela. The coin. The señales. What—”




